docs/workflow/_portable/02-handoff-and-state.md:94:### 2.6 Status Panel write-authority matrix
docs/workflow/_portable/02-handoff-and-state.md:96:Status Panel writes are **role-scoped** — only one role may flip each value. This is the anti-echo-chamber backbone:
docs/workflow/_portable/02-handoff-and-state.md:102:| `READY_FOR_VERIFY` | `feature-build` / `feature-auto-build` |
docs/workflow/_portable/02-handoff-and-state.md:109:**Read-only gates never write Status:** orchestrator/loop agents and any hook scripts only *read* the Status Panel — they BLOCK on a non-expected status, they never write it. A reviewer writes the review verdict; a verify agent writes the verify verdict; the main session is read-only on the Status Panel.
docs/workflow/_portable/02-handoff-and-state.md:125:- **Files Changed** — count + key files (when there are changes; a reviewer writing back the Status Panel must explicitly list `dev_log.md (Status Panel flipped to <value>)`)
docs/workflow/_portable/02-handoff-and-state.md:127:- **State Verification** — the subagent's hard evidence that it read the state file (see §4)
docs/workflow/_portable/02-handoff-and-state.md:135:- **A) Manual mode**: `feature-build` / `bug-fix` (one phase / single step, needs human confirmation)
docs/workflow/_portable/02-handoff-and-state.md:141:**Next Step format:** use a natural-language instruction, e.g. `Start the feature-build agent for billing.`, so it works as a prompt in any tool.
docs/workflow/_portable/02-handoff-and-state.md:151:| No free-form question at the end | e.g. "Should I start feature-build?" replaces the Handoff block |
docs/workflow/_portable/02-handoff-and-state.md:219:Every subagent, before exiting, must `Read` the state file it claims to update or depend on (typically `dev_log.md`), and paste the real on-disk Status Panel values into this field. This is the subagent's hard proof that it "did not just verbally report — it actually read the disk".
